<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Taksin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1734">1734</a>–<a href="/years/1782">1782</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as King Taksin, Taksin the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Taksin was a Siamese general of Chinese descent who rallied resistance after the Burmese destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767 and was crowned king at Thonburi. In a series of campaigns he drove out the Burmese and reunified Siam, bringing rival regimes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> under his rule. Deposed in 1782 amid accusations of erratic behaviour, he was executed, and his general Chakri became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/taksin.jpg" alt="Taksin" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tmd · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Taksin_%26_Phraya_Pichai_Dabhak_Monument_in_Wat_Kungtapao_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
